1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:14,699
This is the docent. A reviewer or an examiner asks it about a paper or a thesis, and it answers with the exact place in the work: the chapter, the figure, the script.

2
00:00:15,099 --> 00:00:26,399
Every answer names the place in the work it rests on, and quotes the sentence. When a claim has code, it re-runs it before reporting the number.

3
00:00:26,399 --> 00:00:42,900
When the work does not address the question, it says Absent, and names the nearest thing the work does cover. Every question and answer is kept in a log, part of the record of the examination.

4
00:00:43,299 --> 00:00:58,200
It works for both sides. For a reviewer or an examiner, it answers questions about the work. For the author, it helps build the package: the work with each claim linked to its code and data.

5
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:16,700
It points at the work and leaves the judging to others: the adversary audits the method, and the examiner decides. The institution's own steps and deadlines come from the clerk. It works offline, because a thesis under examination is not public.

6
00:01:17,099 --> 00:01:26,399
A real exchange, on a made-up thesis. The examiner asks where it shows that serum enlarges colonies, and whether that result holds.

7
00:01:26,399 --> 00:01:40,099
The docent names the chapter and the figure, re-runs the script, and gets the same numbers. It also says what the text leaves out, and who should judge the test.

8
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:52,199
Asked about incubation temperature, which the thesis never mentions, it answers Absent, lists where it looked, and names the nearest thing the thesis does cover.

9
00:01:52,599 --> 00:02:06,699
To use it, open the package in Claude Code and type your question to the docent. Give it the question in plain words; it works out whether you are the examiner or the author.

10
00:02:06,699 --> 00:02:26,099
The first line is the verdict: Located, Absent, or Incomplete when the package itself is missing something. The detail gives the places and the re-run. Each package carries its own note on where things are, and the docent reads it first.
